Subject: WPE on Phoenix: zero-copy HEVC frames in the FFmpeg media player (rpivid's GPU buffers)

On top of 0030/0031 (platform/graphics/ffmpeg/, BSD-2-Clause): with the video_player port's
hevc_rpivid, the rpivid block decodes each picture into a V3D BO of the render server and the
decoder returns that buffer as an AV_PIX_FMT_DRM_PRIME frame (libavcodec/rpivid_drm.h:
DRM_FORMAT_NV12, DRM_FORMAT_MOD_BROADCOM_SAND128_COL_HEIGHT). The compositor imports each
buffer once as an EGLImage (EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import_modifiers) and draws it with
TextureMapper::drawTextureExternalOES: Mesa's v3d de-tiles it on the GPU and converts the
colours (the frame's colour space and range as EGL hints). This replaces the CPU SAND->planar
copy in the decoder (6.3 ms per 1080p picture on the Pi 4) and the three glTexImage2D uploads
in the compositor (~5 ms, tiled by Mesa on the CPU). Coordination repo:
docs/gpu-new-lane/M10b-video-zero-copy.md.

  CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferFFmpeg  requestZeroCopy(): before avcodec_open2 of hevc_rpivid,
                                rpivid_out=drm_prime and slice (not frame) threads, once the
                                render node's buffer operations are installed
                                (rpivid_bo_drm_install(), librpivid_bo_drm.a) and the display
                                has EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import_modifiers.
                                A DRM_PRIME frame is drawn from a per-compositing-thread cache
                                of EGLImages + GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES textures, one per buffer
                                (named by its dma-buf's st_dev/st_ino, the BO handle; entries
                                idle for 3 s are dropped): a texture per picture would make Mesa
                                allocate a ~3 MB tiled shadow every frame. The layer buffer
                                holds the frame until the compositor drops it, which keeps the
                                decoder from writing the buffer (it also waits for the GPU's last
                                read). An import failure, or Skia painting (canvas, snapshots),
                                takes the CPU readback (rpivid_drm_frame_to_planar). Ordinary
                                frames take the upload path as before, frame by frame: after a
                                block failure the decoder goes on with system-memory frames.
  MediaPlayerPrivateFFmpeg      asks for it; "decoder ... zero_copy=0|1"; the stat line adds
                                zc=<the last picture presented was a GPU buffer> and
                                zc_painted=<pictures drawn that way>; upload_ms stays the
                                compositor's CPU time per painted picture (zero copy: the cache
                                lookup or the one-time import, and the draw)

WPE_PHOENIX_MEDIA_ZERO_COPY=1 turns it on (opt-in: build 60 painted fewer frames with it). Without hevc_rpivid's zero-copy API in the
FFmpeg prefix (no libavcodec/rpivid_drm.h or rpivid_bo_drm.h) the code compiles to the old path.

